## 11. Parent Inventory and Stock Movement Rules

The system must have one consistent source of truth for inventory.

The inventory balance must be based on recorded stock movements rather than independently maintained, contradictory parent and child balances.

Use the existing inventory architecture if it can correctly support these rules. Otherwise, implement the smallest necessary changes.

The following rules are mandatory:

1. A normal parent-product receipt increases the parent stock.
2. An exceptional child-product receipt increases its associated parent stock.
3. An outgoing child product decreases its associated parent stock.
4. A child product does not receive an independent stock balance.
5. Every stock movement retains its actual product, resolved parent product, weight, date, operator, and relevant document references.
6. Stock changes must be atomic: an invalid transaction must not partially update inventory.
7. Historical transactions must remain available for reporting and auditing.
8. Parent inventory must never be accidentally counted twice.

Review all existing stock calculations, inventory queries, product availability rules, and report logic to ensure they use the same model.

If the project stores stock balances separately for performance, ensure that the balance is updated transactionally and can be reconciled with the movement history.

Do not introduce an unnecessarily complicated inventory ledger if the existing architecture can be safely extended.

